Subject: ubox: write the boot's backlog to the persistent log file, and respawn its writer

The logfile follower asks logd for no backlog, so the persistent log
file starts each boot at the moment the follower attached and misses
the kernel and early-init lines before it. At boot, ask for the whole
ring buffer, which then holds this boot alone.

The follower also has no respawn, so a follower that dies leaves the
log file unwritten until the next reboot. Respawn it. procd respawns
with the argv it first started, so the boot instance asks for the
backlog through a once-per-boot marker beside its pid file: a respawned
follower resumes without writing the ring buffer a second time.

Start9 downstream patch (start-technologies projects/start-wrt).
Applies to: openwrt v25.12.6

--- a/package/system/ubox/files/log.init
+++ b/package/system/ubox/files/log.init
@@ -62,8 +62,15 @@
 	mkdir -p "$(dirname "${log_file}")"
 
 	procd_open_instance logfile
-	procd_set_param command "$PROG" -f -F "$log_file" -p "$pid_file"
+	if [ "$_BOOT" = "1" ]; then
+		procd_set_param command /bin/sh -c \
+			'[ -e "$0" ] || { : > "$0"; set -- "$@" -l 1000000; }; exec "$@"' \
+			"${pid_file%.pid}.backlog" "$PROG" -f -F "$log_file" -p "$pid_file"
+	else
+		procd_set_param command "$PROG" -f -F "$log_file" -p "$pid_file"
+	fi
 	[ -n "${log_size}" ] && procd_append_param command -S "$log_size"
+	procd_set_param respawn
 	procd_close_instance
 }
 
